The universal selector (*) applies box-sizing: border-box to every element in this example. This means the panel's width includes its padding and border instead of adding them outside the declared width.

The panel uses width: 100% and max-width: 320px. It can use the available width on a narrow screen, but it will not grow beyond 320 pixels. The body's padding also creates space around the page content.

In more complex layouts, box sizing works together with Flexbox and Grid. If a row of cards is too wide, inspect the declared widths, padding, borders, margins, and container spacing. Avoid fixing every overflow problem by randomly reducing widths. Identify which part of the box model is causing the extra space.

A useful starting point for many projects is:

* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

This makes width calculations more predictable, but it does not automatically make every layout responsive. You still need sensible widths, flexible containers, and appropriate layout rules.
